  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nA low pressure system over the Southern High Plains today will begin \nbreaking down and shearing out. As the wave stretches to the east \nand is absorbed in the flow, it will drag a cold frontal boundary \nfrom Texas to the Carolinas over the weekend. Pooled moisture along \nthe front will support heavy rain, but the primary surge of 2+ PWATs \nwill arrive from the south with an easterly wave Friday night into \nSaturday. The combination of mainly westerly flow, ascent along the \nflank of the subtropical jet stream, and anomalously high moisture \ncontent will result in heavy rain for parts of the broader Gulf \nCoast region. Guidance is in agreement that heaviest amounts through \nthe weekend will likely be close to the Gulf Coast itself. Average \ntotals will be near 0.75 inches in the Delta region and closer to 2 \ninches in the Pine Belt region. Where showers and storms fall over \nthe same areas, upper end forecasts suggest 3 to 4 inches of rain \ncould fall through the weekend. At this time, no widespread flash \nflooding is expected in our area, but will monitor trends in \nguidance.\n\nBy Monday, however, a surging polar cold front will sweep into the \nMid-South and Gulf Coast. A breezy and cooling north wind will \npersist through the day Monday and chase the decreasing rain \nchances. A 1025mb surface high moving from the Canadian Prairies \ntoward the Mid-Atlantic is around the 90th percentile in strength \nfor this time of year. We won't receive the full force of this high, \nbut cooler temps will still be felt! Low temperatures Monday night \nand onward look to range from the 50s to near 60 degrees. These \nvalues would be about 5-15 degrees below normal for early October. \n/NF/",
